  "prompt_text": "The figure links Antarctica’s climate system to a summary of the continent’s key physical features. At the top, Antarctica sits at the center of global atmospheric and ocean circulation. Broad wind belts are shown encircling the Southern Hemisphere, with trade winds, westerlies, and polar easterlies driving movement of air masses. Cold air and katabatic winds appear to flow outward from the ice sheet, while warmer air is associated with lower latitudes. Ocean arrows show a large contrast between warm surface water and cold deep water, emphasizing the Antarctic Circumpolar Current and zones of deep-water formation around the continent. Together, these panels convey Antarctica’s central role in redistributing heat and in global thermohaline circulation.\n\nThe lower panel summarizes major geographic and environmental characteristics of Antarctica. It highlights the continent’s huge share of global ice and freshwater, the vast ice sheet area and thickness, and an extremely small ice-free fraction. Other callouts point to notable landmarks and extremes, including Lake Vostok beneath the ice, the Vestfold Hills, Mt Vinson, Mt Erebus, the McMurdo Dry Valleys, the Lambert Glacier, and a very large iceberg. The map also notes Antarctica’s severe climate, low rainfall, and extensive winter sea ice.",
